item.text:
**Reused evidence**
None directly reused for 6B. The prior reusable items were not strong enough on their own to count as welfare evidence for this chapter.

**New evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"State of the Nation Address of His Excellency Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr. President of the Philippines to the Congress of the Philippines","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/presidential-speech/state-of-the-nation-address-of-his-excellency-ferdinand-r-marcos-jr-president-of-the-philippines-to-the-congress-of-the-philippines/","claim":"Marcos presented human welfare as a governing priority, saying the DSWD would lead support for people most in need, that no one would be left behind, and that the administration would use social-protection programs for those needing help.","locator":"first SONA passages on social protection, vulnerable sectors, and DSWD-led assistance","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential speech text is strong evidence of policy intent and stated priorities, though not of delivery by itself.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct target-year speech delivered on July 25, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos as his own policy statement within presidential authority.","contrary_evidence":["This is a speech of intent, not proof of execution.","It relies on promised programs rather than audited outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.7","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"State of the Nation Address of His Excellency Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr. President of the Philippines to the Congress of the Philippines","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/presidential-speech/state-of-the-nation-address-of-his-excellency-ferdinand-r-marcos-jr-president-of-the-philippines-to-the-congress-of-the-philippines/","claim":"Marcos said food-price pressures would be met with financial and technical support to farmers, cheaper farm inputs, fuel subsidies, loans, and aid to qualified beneficiaries, explicitly linking agricultural policy to consumer purchasing power and food security.","locator":"food-supply and agriculture section of the speech","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary speech text is strong evidence of the stated policy approach and target groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct target-year policy statement in July 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos; he is setting the agenda and naming the intended beneficiaries.","contrary_evidence":["Announced support does not show the eventual effectiveness of the measures.","Some interventions may have been short-term price relief rather than durable welfare reform."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"State of the Nation Address of His Excellency Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr. President of the Philippines to the Congress of the Philippines","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/presidential-speech/state-of-the-nation-address-of-his-excellency-ferdinand-r-marcos-jr-president-of-the-philippines-to-the-congress-of-the-philippines/","claim":"Marcos directed the DSWD to respond quickly to calamity victims and crises, preposition family food packs and non-food essentials before disasters, add warehouses and operations centers, and coordinate emergency shelter assistance with housing agencies.","locator":"disaster-response section of the speech","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary speech text is strong evidence of the crisis-response directive and institutional setup.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct target-year speech and administration directive in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos as an instruction to the executive branch under his authority.","contrary_evidence":["This is a directive, not proof of post-disaster effectiveness.","Disaster response still depends heavily on agency and local implementation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Overcoming Poverty and Inequality in the Philippines : Past, Present, and Prospects for the Future","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-11-23","url":"https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099325011232224571","claim":"The World Bank reported long-run poverty reduction in the Philippines, with the poverty rate falling from 49.2 percent in 1985 to 16.7 percent in 2018, the middle class expanding to nearly 12 million people, and the economically secure population rising to 44 million.","locator":"report summary / document metadata and opening summary text","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The World Bank is a strong independent baseline source, but this is a structural history report rather than a Marcos-specific evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Structural baseline and inherited welfare context relevant to the start of Marcos's term.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited-baseline context only; the improvement described predates Marcos and cannot be credited to him directly.","contrary_evidence":["It does not evaluate Marcos's 2022 execution.","The baseline itself shows persistent inequality, so it is not proof of adequate welfare performance."],"lenses":["6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO supports regions to increase COVID-19 vaccination uptake among seniors, indigent population","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-04-02","url":"https://www.who.int/philippines/news/feature-stories/detail/who-supports-regions-increase-covid-19-vaccination-uptake-seniors-indigent-population","claim":"WHO said the Philippines still had around 2.4 million senior citizens who had not received even one COVID-19 dose and that WHO was supporting DOH efforts to improve vaccination coverage among vulnerable seniors, GIDA residents, and other hard-to-reach groups.","locator":"opening paragraphs on priority groups and vaccination gaps","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO reporting is a strong independent source for the health-access gap and the priority populations involved.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year baseline and early Marcos-period health context in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly inherited and implementation context; the source does not by itself prove Marcos-directed change, but it frames the vulnerable groups his administration inherited.","contrary_evidence":["The source emphasizes an ongoing access gap rather than solved access.","It does not isolate Marcos-era causation by itself."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO, EU support the Philippines’ vaccine information system","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-08-23","url":"https://www.who.int/philippines/news/detail/23-08-2022-who-eu-support-philippines--vaccine-information-system-vims","claim":"WHO said DOH and DICT were jointly maintaining the national Vaccine Information Management System to guide decision-makers in improving access to immunisation services, with WHO supporting capacity building and long-term integration.","locator":"opening paragraphs on VIMS and decision-making","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO is a strong independent source for the system description and its stated purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year health-system improvement within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to the Marcos administration through the DOH and DICT, but the source also reflects partner support and system-level capacity building.","contrary_evidence":["The article does not measure final health outcomes.","It is about information systems rather than direct service delivery."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DOH achieves major strides in fight vs COVID-19, strengthens PH healthcare system","publisher":"Department of Health","publication_date":"2022-10-07","url":"https://caro.doh.gov.ph/doh-achieves-major-strides-in-fight-vs-covid-19-strengthens-ph-healthcare-system/","claim":"The DOH said it had accelerated COVID-19 vaccination and booster rollout, sustained testing, strengthened detection and isolation systems, and reinvigorated Universal Health Care safety nets for poor and vulnerable groups in accordance with President Marcos's first-100-days directives.","locator":"opening paragraphs and list of actions under Marcos directives","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official health-department release is strong for the policy actions described, though not independently for effectiveness.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct target-year 2022 implementation under Marcos's first 100 days.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable in part to Marcos because the release explicitly ties the actions to his directives.","contrary_evidence":["The release is self-reported by the implementing agency.","It describes actions and system strengthening rather than measured outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DepEd releases guidelines for School Year 2022-2023","publisher":"Department of Education","publication_date":"2022-07-12","url":"https://www.deped.gov.ph/2022/07/12/deped-releases-guidelines-for-school-year-2022-2023/","claim":"DepEd said the school calendar was issued in line with its commitment to resume five days of in-person classes, with all public and private schools transitioning back to in-person learning by November 2, 2022.","locator":"opening paragraphs on school calendar and transition back to in-person classes","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official education guidance is strong evidence for the policy choice and timetable.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year education policy enacted in July 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Marcos administration through the education department, though the order itself was signed by the vice president and education secretary.","contrary_evidence":["This is a reopening and delivery measure, not evidence of learning gains.","Implementation quality could still vary by school and region."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.5","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF celebrates learners’ first day back in school","publisher":"UNICEF Philippines","publication_date":"2022-08-22","url":"https://www.unicef.org/philippines/press-releases/unicef-celebrates-learners-first-day-back-school","claim":"UNICEF said getting children back into classrooms was the first step to addressing the learning crisis and praised the country's move back to in-person schooling as part of learning recovery.","locator":"headline and opening quote paragraphs","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF is a strong independent source for education-recovery framing, though the source is supportive and does not measure outcome impact by itself.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-year education-recovery context in August 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly administration context rather than direct Marcos authorship; it supports the broader policy environment but not sole ruler credit.","contrary_evidence":["The source is normative and supportive rather than an audit of results.","It does not isolate the Marcos administration from inherited learning losses."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Japan and UNICEF provide educational tablets to learners in Region VIII for learning recovery","publisher":"UNICEF Philippines","publication_date":"2022-07-29","url":"https://www.unicef.org/philippines/press-releases/japan-and-unicef-provide-educational-tablets-learners-region-viii-learning-recovery","claim":"UNICEF and Japan turned over 400 educational tablets to support at least 800 non-readers and struggling readers in Eastern Visayas as part of learning recovery efforts.","locator":"opening paragraph describing tablets and target learners","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"The claim is concrete and specific, though it is a partner-supported intervention rather than a full-government outcome evaluation.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-year learning-recovery context in July 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly relevant to Marcos-era education recovery because it sits within the national learning-recovery environment, but the source is not a direct Marcos order.","contrary_evidence":["This is a localized partner intervention, not a nationwide Marcos policy result.","It shows need and response, not macro-level educational improvement."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"DBM releases billions of pesos to aid rice farmers, vulnerable households, calamity victims, commuters, healthcare workers in the first 100 days of Marcos Jr Administration","publisher":"Department of Budget and Management","publication_date":"2022-10-08","url":"https://www.dbm.gov.ph/index.php/management-2/405-dbm-releases-billions-of-pesos-to-aid-rice-farmers-vulnerable-households-calamity-victims-commuters-healthcare-workers-in-the-first-100-days-of-marcos-jr-administration","claim":"In Marcos's first 100 days, DBM said it approved billions in assistance for rice farmers, the most vulnerable households affected by inflation, calamity victims, commuters, and healthcare workers, including P5,000 subsidies for more than 1.5 million rice farmers.","locator":"opening paragraphs and subsidy details in the release","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official budget-management release is strong evidence for the program rollout and intended beneficiaries.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year and Marcos's first 100 days in office.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Marcos administration because the release describes first-100-days policy execution under his presidency.","contrary_evidence":["It is an administrative release, not an independent audit of effectiveness.","The aid is responsive relief, not structural poverty reduction by itself."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.7","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PBBM vows full support to ‘Paeng’ victims; eyes long-term solution to Cavite flooding problem","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-10-31","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/news_releases/pbbm-vows-full-support-to-paeng-victims-eyes-long-term-solution-to-cavite-flooding-problem/","claim":"Marcos pledged support for Typhoon Paeng victims, distributed financial aid through DSWD's AICS program, and said he wanted a long-term solution to the flooding problem.","locator":"opening paragraphs on aid distribution and long-term flood solution","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government release is strong for the fact of the aid distribution and the president's stated response.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year disaster response in October 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos because he personally led the aid distribution and situation briefing.","contrary_evidence":["It shows emergency response, not durable resilience outcomes.","The main evidence is the president's own account of the response."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PBBM declares state of calamity in ‘Paeng’-hit regions","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-11-02","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/news_releases/pbbm-declares-state-of-calamity-in-paeng-hit-regions/","claim":"Marcos placed several regions under a state of calamity after Severe Tropical Storm Paeng, directing agencies to continue rescue, relief, rehabilitation, and price-control measures and to augment basic services in affected areas.","locator":"opening paragraphs and proclamation summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential release is strong for the decision and its stated purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year disaster-response decision in November 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos as head of state exercising calamity powers.","contrary_evidence":["The declaration is a response measure, not proof of successful recovery.","Its effect depends on later implementation by agencies and LGUs."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PBBM signs P5.2 trillion 2023 national budget","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/news_releases/pbbm-signs-p5-2-trillion-2023-national-budget/","claim":"Marcos signed the 2023 national budget in December 2022 and identified education, public works, health, social welfare, agriculture, and transportation as the priority sectors of his administration's first national budget.","locator":"opening paragraphs on the 2023 budget and priority sectors","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary budget-signing release is strong evidence for the administration's stated spending priorities and legislative support.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year action at the end of 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos because he signed and described the first budget of his administration.","contrary_evidence":["Budget priority is not the same as actual service delivery.","Spending outcomes depend on execution and procurement after enactment."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Recovery, Growth, and Sustainability Highlighted in the Signed FY 2022 National Budget","publisher":"Department of Budget and Management","publication_date":"2021-12-30","url":"https://www.dbm.gov.ph/index.php/management-2/462-recovery-growth-and-sustainability-highlighted-in-the-signed-fy-2022-national-budget","claim":"The FY 2022 budget, inherited by Marcos when he took office, gave the biggest allocations to education, health, and social welfare and funded health-insurance premiums for 13.2 million indigent families and 7.3 million senior citizens.","locator":"budget-allocation table and social-protection paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official budget documentation is strong for the inherited baseline, though it predates Marcos's term.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline relevant to the start of Marcos's 2022 term.","ruler_attribution":"Inherited baseline only; this budget was signed before Marcos became president.","contrary_evidence":["Because it predates Marcos, it cannot be counted as his own performance.","It shows starting conditions rather than ruler-specific delivery."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.9","6B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I screened roughly 30 to 35 documents across PCO, DBM, DOH, DepEd, WHO, UNICEF, the World Bank, and related official releases.

I kept the strongest non-duplicative items that showed:
- 2022 welfare intent and social-protection framing in Marcos's first SONA
- 2022 disaster response and inflation relief
- 2022 health-system, vaccination, and education-reopening measures
- 2022 budget-signing priorities and first-100-days assistance
- inherited baseline context on poverty, inequality, and the pre-Marcos budget

I rejected or de-emphasized:
- 2023 to 2026 follow-up articles that did not directly describe 2022 actions, except where they only clarified the 2022 rollout
- duplicate mirrors of the same PCO or Reuters-syndicated content
- partisan praise pieces that repeated budget or welfare claims without adding distinct evidence
- inherited-program pages that predated Marcos without a clean attribution bridge to his presidency
- purely macro or baseline indicators with no ruler-linked welfare choice or implementation detail

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct, independent outcome evidence is still thin for how much Marcos's 2022 actions improved actual household welfare, nutrition, learning, or health access versus simply continuing inherited programs.
- Household-level distribution data by region, income class, and vulnerable group would help distinguish broad intent from realized reach.
- More audit-style evidence would help on whether PhilSys, CBMS, and the budget priorities improved targeting and service delivery by end-2022.
- More third-party evidence would help separate Marcos-era contribution from inherited post-pandemic reopening and donor-supported recovery.
